   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "The hissing sounds",
     "texto": [
      "**Ж** sounds **zh**, like the «s» in «measure». **Ш** sounds **sh**. **Щ** is a **longer, softer sh**, which we will write «shch». **Ч** sounds **ch**. **Ц** sounds **ts**."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Vowels with a «y» sound",
     "texto": [
      "**Я** sounds roughly **ya**, **Ю**, **yu**, and **Ё**, **yo**; the **ё** always carries the stress.",
      "**Е** can also start with a short y sound: at the start of a word and after a vowel, **ь** or **ъ** usually sounds «ye»; after a consonant, the vowel gets close to e and softens that consonant.",
      "**Я**, **Ю** and **Ё** behave in a similar way: after a consonant (with no separating sign) they soften it; at the start or after a vowel, **ь** or **ъ** add the y sound."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "The rarest letter",
     "texto": [
      "**Ы** does not exist in English. It is an **i said with the tongue pulled back**, as if someone were squeezing your throat as you say it. We will write it **y**: **ты** (you) = «tih», **мы** (we) = «mih»."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Two letters that make no sound",
     "texto": [
      "**Ь** has no sound of its own and usually shows that the consonant before it is soft. **нь** sounds almost like «ny»: **день** (day) = «dyen». After **ж, ш, ч, щ** it does not change the pronunciation: in **ночь**, for example, **ч** is already soft.",
      "**Ъ** is not pronounced. In words with a prefix, it separates the final consonant of the prefix from **е, ё, ю, я**: the consonant stays hard and a short «y» appears before the vowel, as in **подъезд** (entrance to a building)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "The letter Ё in real texts",
     "texto": [
      "**Ё** always carries the stress and is pronounced «yo». In study materials it is usually written with its two dots; in many ordinary texts the dots are left out and you will see **е** in its place.",
      "Learn to recognise both forms: **ёлка / елка** (fir tree). Context helps you tell apart words such as **всё** (everything) and **все** (everyone)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
